WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.660
For most of its history, life on Earth was microbial.

2
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:14.100
Rocks 3.48 billion years old in Western Australia hold what researchers read as

3
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.080
traces of microbes in hot springs.

4
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.920
Then life remade the planet.

5
00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.960
Oxygen began to build up in the air by about 2.4 billion years ago.

6
00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:32.380
Many-celled algae appear just over a billion years ago, animals by about 558

7
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.340
million, and plants on land by about 470 million.

8
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:41.040
And five times, mass extinctions swept away most species.

9
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:47.063
The Great Oxidation Event was the first big buildup of oxygen in the air.

10
00:00:48.303 --> 00:00:51.303
It was not the start of oxygen-making photosynthesis.

11
00:00:52.663 --> 00:00:55.303
With oxygen, aerobic metabolism spread.

12
00:00:56.583 --> 00:01:01.183
An ozone layer would have shielded life near the surface from ultraviolet light.

13
00:01:02.363 --> 00:01:05.123
And living things had to cope with oxygen's harm.

14
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:11.213
A mass extinction is the loss of more than three quarters of species in a

15
00:01:11.213 --> 00:01:12.593
geologically short time.

16
00:01:13.853 --> 00:01:18.933
At the end of the Cretaceous, 66 million years ago, the evidence converged.

17
00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:25.273
Iridium, rare in Earth's crust but common in many meteorites, is enriched at the

18
00:01:25.273 --> 00:01:27.413
boundary at widely separated sites.

19
00:01:28.633 --> 00:01:31.433
A buried crater, Chicxulub, lies in Mexico.

20
00:01:32.433 --> 00:01:36.533
Dating puts impact and extinction within 32,000 years.

21
00:01:37.873 --> 00:01:39.413
The impact triggered the extinction.

22
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:46.847
The end of the Permian, about 252 million years ago, was the largest loss in the

23
00:01:46.847 --> 00:01:51.747
marine record, about 81 percent of marine species by one estimate.

24
00:01:53.107 --> 00:01:58.027
At Meishan in China, dated ash beds show the dying there took about 60 thousand

25
00:01:58.027 --> 00:02:00.027
years, with wide uncertainty.

26
00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:02.987
That is a clock for one place.

27
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:06.227
Any proposed cause has to fit it.

28
00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.250
Extinctions also open space.

29
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:15.030
Placental mammals diversified mainly after the end-Cretaceous extinction.

30
00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:20.030
That timing fits release from competitors, but does not prove it.

31
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.570
A flask can test the logic.

32
00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:29.330
Rainey and Travisano grew one genotype of the bacterium Pseudomonas in still broth.

33
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.850
New heritable types filled different zones.

34
00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.830
In shaken broth, they did not.

35
00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.790
These were new types, not new species.

36
00:02:39.983 --> 00:02:41.123
How do we know when?

37
00:02:42.363 --> 00:02:48.163
Zircon in volcanic ash is a mineral clock: uranium decays to lead at a steady rate.

38
00:02:49.523 --> 00:02:52.403
Ash beds above and below a fossil bracket its age.

39
00:02:53.523 --> 00:02:55.583
But the oldest fossil is only a floor.

40
00:02:57.043 --> 00:03:01.323
Life existed by 3.48 billion years ago; it may be older.

41
00:03:02.416 --> 00:03:07.476
So the history of life is transitions that remade the planet, extinctions that

42
00:03:07.476 --> 00:03:13.096
cleared it, and radiations that filled it again, each dated by rocks, with fossil

43
00:03:13.096 --> 00:03:15.516
dates telling you only when, at the latest.
